[{:db/id "q-256516/field-1/9b32bd1c2d85515eb67bf6ed1aa42ff7a66b88544eaafce7b11ad994fbde1d11" :answer/id #uuid "b4eabb9e-b68d-5972-9903-57a657b710e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256516/field-1/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" :answer/id #uuid "29a697e3-b8ba-5afc-b03d-5235862609bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256516/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "11f3e810-4ede-5e78-9e36-76c496eb2b5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256516/field-1/9b32bd1c2d85515eb67bf6ed1aa42ff7a66b88544eaafce7b11ad994fbde1d11" :answer-field/choices ["q-256516/field-1/9b32bd1c2d85515eb67bf6ed1aa42ff7a66b88544eaafce7b11ad994fbde1d11" "q-256516/field-1/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256516" :question/id #uuid "17534113-1fc4-51b5-9d1a-d27396a68277" :question/math-academy-id "q-256516" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x & y \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2(1)(1) & 3(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)(1)-1 \\\\ (1)^{3}-(1)^{2}(1)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.5 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-256516/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256516"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-256561/field-1/27b57d7515c07d3dbc81141e28382be23c609819f2e016404ba73918efe7bfef" :answer/id #uuid "30726d1e-a908-5452-82e7-d8accb1afdf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256561/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1fdc7b0a-ad03-50ce-b26b-0a19a4c92ba8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256561/field-1/27b57d7515c07d3dbc81141e28382be23c609819f2e016404ba73918efe7bfef" :answer-field/choices ["q-256561/field-1/27b57d7515c07d3dbc81141e28382be23c609819f2e016404ba73918efe7bfef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256561/field-2/bd7856476083ec72977fd8ba59fbe8a505406ebf05338e8cce32bccf9a5589b9" :answer/id #uuid "bd1f01da-5d95-556c-9e61-139a21eda50f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.767" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256561/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c0a2dc-667b-5eb9-b4a1-ba1cec3ff885" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256561/field-2/bd7856476083ec72977fd8ba59fbe8a505406ebf05338e8cce32bccf9a5589b9" :answer-field/choices ["q-256561/field-2/bd7856476083ec72977fd8ba59fbe8a505406ebf05338e8cce32bccf9a5589b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256561" :question/id #uuid "6f454d13-77bf-5f63-8f88-c5866fdc5354" :question/math-academy-id "q-256561" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & 3(2)^{2} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}-3(2) & -3(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}+(2)^{3}-6 \\\\ (1)^{3}-3(1)(2)+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-3)-12\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} \\\\ \\frac{53}{30}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.9 \\\\ 1.767\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-256561/field-1" "q-256561/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256561"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-256200/field-1/99ec22667d7beb133a4518308901d9e9ff77fca255a67f5b052651a3d4ae32bc" :answer/id #uuid "75b224a4-bb0d-560a-813d-2a19574dc8fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.083" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256200/field-1/41b75074407fce27f13146588cd10c1c7d3e4918571d6df4ff8b3e80f1d07226" :answer/id #uuid "ce119a2c-de45-5733-bcd4-ca3725a92c59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.917" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186202954 :answer-field/correct "q-256200/field-1/99ec22667d7beb133a4518308901d9e9ff77fca255a67f5b052651a3d4ae32bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-256200/field-1/99ec22667d7beb133a4518308901d9e9ff77fca255a67f5b052651a3d4ae32bc" "q-256200/field-1/41b75074407fce27f13146588cd10c1c7d3e4918571d6df4ff8b3e80f1d07226"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-256200"] :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [-2,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-0.917$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 3{y}^{4} & 3{y}^{2}+12x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 3{y}^{4} & 3{y}^{2}+12x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 3{y}^{4} & 3{y}^{2}+12x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{4}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3(-1)^{4} & 3(-1)^{2}+12(-1)(-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1)(-1)-1 \\\\ (-1)^{3}+3(-1)(-1)^{4}+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 15-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{12} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.083 \\\\ -0.917\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-256557/field-1/96664beb16f3274f7efe1c03568405390ba60899a420620ed76fdb4c8b73e15c" :answer/id #uuid "16ff7f43-e176-5979-b5dd-1cf804cad1be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.544" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256557/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0702b077-82b2-5e59-8bc2-0af9f344d1ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256557/field-1/96664beb16f3274f7efe1c03568405390ba60899a420620ed76fdb4c8b73e15c" :answer-field/choices ["q-256557/field-1/96664beb16f3274f7efe1c03568405390ba60899a420620ed76fdb4c8b73e15c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256557/field-2/d8ab38be3f49642d1a4658b9776ac4014cadb1194d30d4103163e0633badeda8" :answer/id #uuid "183ffa84-34c2-5b6a-94d4-5c78977f8ff4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.883" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256557/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f733a2a1-f06d-570f-81cb-760fc5a3ace9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256557/field-2/d8ab38be3f49642d1a4658b9776ac4014cadb1194d30d4103163e0633badeda8" :answer-field/choices ["q-256557/field-2/d8ab38be3f49642d1a4658b9776ac4014cadb1194d30d4103163e0633badeda8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256557" :question/id #uuid "ab80d1d1-0dd0-5412-9fb6-abe4a7b26239" :question/math-academy-id "q-256557" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [-1,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(2)(-1)^{3}+1 & 3(2)^{2}(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\ 3(2)^{2} & -4(-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2 \\\\ (2)^{3}-(-1)^{4}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 14 \\\\ 12 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 4-14\\cdot 12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -14 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{180}[\\begin{aligned}-82 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{139}{90} \\\\ -\\frac{53}{60}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.544 \\\\ -0.883\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-256557/field-1" "q-256557/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256557"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-256718/field-1/b86fd7910455a486bae5ba18c3ea4713fd2863bd1650a094a02ee0b765a6a656" :answer/id #uuid "4ed7cdd8-7d86-52c6-9977-a3fe99a3716f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.167" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256718/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9659b1-c765-5483-a796-12aa8ae8c8af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256718/field-1/b86fd7910455a486bae5ba18c3ea4713fd2863bd1650a094a02ee0b765a6a656" :answer-field/choices ["q-256718/field-1/b86fd7910455a486bae5ba18c3ea4713fd2863bd1650a094a02ee0b765a6a656"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256718/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "38d14730-9c6e-586f-9b51-5808de3f665e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256718/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "92d3e0ca-a2fb-5163-8de8-2bb4d9de6eaa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256718/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-256718/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256718" :question/id #uuid "bed99fc1-5ccd-5215-9718-d009510b2e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-256718" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(3) & -2(2) \\\\ 2(3) & 2(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}-(2)^{2}-4 \\\\ (3)^{2}+(2)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}12 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{12\\cdot 4-(-4)\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}2.167 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-256718/field-1" "q-256718/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256718"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-256229/field-1/e82fd555f66b4a53bb72a07b6a1dbaf2eaec15fb5157c82d3b64be8003154b4b" :answer/id #uuid "e61f1e20-4d34-5464-a868-43a2d3b7c26a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.833" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186202956 :answer-field/correct "q-256229/field-1/e82fd555f66b4a53bb72a07b6a1dbaf2eaec15fb5157c82d3b64be8003154b4b" :answer-field/choices ["q-256229/field-1/e82fd555f66b4a53bb72a07b6a1dbaf2eaec15fb5157c82d3b64be8003154b4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256229/field-2/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" :answer/id #uuid "95dad497-01e8-5588-bedb-65c01245c8ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186202957 :answer-field/correct "q-256229/field-2/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" :answer-field/choices ["q-256229/field-2/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-256229"] :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-xy-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-xy-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-x+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-x+y-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(2)-(1) & 1-(2) \\\\ 2(2)-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}+(1)-(2)(1)-3 \\\\ (2)^{2}-(2)+(1)-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.833 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-256621/field-1/8b5787e66ba8d37c2f10276a28969fa31556ae378b7255a75d81cc058e28c78a" :answer/id #uuid "85f32812-b1bb-5028-ba73-34718fbdc0f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.773" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256621/field-1/610f1807e70d1baaa4387349de83c44323b91c1e1c9ed39188e21d8b00d4d8ff" :answer/id #uuid "758c7dab-5fec-5659-ad55-d76b5859a6e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.227" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256621/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d78a748-c693-5e3d-9472-8b4bb8119c2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256621/field-1/8b5787e66ba8d37c2f10276a28969fa31556ae378b7255a75d81cc058e28c78a" :answer-field/choices ["q-256621/field-1/8b5787e66ba8d37c2f10276a28969fa31556ae378b7255a75d81cc058e28c78a" "q-256621/field-1/610f1807e70d1baaa4387349de83c44323b91c1e1c9ed39188e21d8b00d4d8ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256621" :question/id #uuid "7eb652cc-ca6c-55c5-956d-e0ca0dacc980" :question/math-academy-id "q-256621" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[2,5]\\times [0,5]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[5,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[5,\\,5]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2.727$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y-20) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y-20)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(5) & 2(5) \\\\ 2(5) & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(5)^{2}+(5)^{2}-25 \\\\ (5)^{2}-5-20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{10\\cdot (-1)-10\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{110}[\\begin{aligned}-25 \\\\ -250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{105}{22} \\\\ \\frac{30}{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}4.773 \\\\ 2.727\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-256621/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256621"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-256603/field-1/618fd6c2d11c278c509eeec51332bd4f91ae66aad65cdf8ddeae8938fd15b22e" :answer/id #uuid "aa346361-7495-50c5-a1a2-f44dc0b19fdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.775" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256603/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "16cead0d-a868-58ea-bcb1-69a2a99132a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256603/field-1/618fd6c2d11c278c509eeec51332bd4f91ae66aad65cdf8ddeae8938fd15b22e" :answer-field/choices ["q-256603/field-1/618fd6c2d11c278c509eeec51332bd4f91ae66aad65cdf8ddeae8938fd15b22e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256603" :question/id #uuid "38753261-3491-5103-8c23-312e2e3be330" :question/math-academy-id "q-256603" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.25,\\,0.25]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $1.095$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+y-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3(0.25) & 3(0.25)^{2}+3(1.25) \\\\ 3(1.25)^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1.25+(0.25)^{3}+3(1.25)(0.25)-4 \\\\ (1.25)^{3}+0.25-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1.75 & 3.9375 \\\\ 4.6875 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1.796875 \\\\ 0.203125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{1.75\\cdot 1-3.9375\\cdot 4.6875}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3.9375 \\\\ -4.6875 & 1.75\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1.796\\,875 \\\\ 0.203\\,125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{16.707\\,031…}[\\begin{aligned}-2.596\\,679… \\\\ 8.778\\,320…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.095 \\\\ 0.775\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-256603/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69"] 