nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[0,\\,4]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-x-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}(4)-1 & (0)^{3} \\\\ (4)^{2} & 2(0)(4)-2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}(4)-0-1 \\\\ (0)(4)^{2}-2(4)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 16}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-256624/field-1" "q-256624/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256624"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-256610/field-1/4133a8c7c1b76441a08439f0370c2c879fc02d55fca4d783728c0082bf3e83ff" :answer/id #uuid "a38f9a39-4f0c-5d1c-a2a5-28cc3a07b14a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.253" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256610/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e431cfd-edf8-5e00-b323-cb88d99ba68e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256610/field-1/4133a8c7c1b76441a08439f0370c2c879fc02d55fca4d783728c0082bf3e83ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-256610/field-1/4133a8c7c1b76441a08439f0370c2c879fc02d55fca4d783728c0082bf3e83ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256610" :question/id #uuid "e952a7bf-b1bc-5392-9ccf-bf41d750ae32" :question/math-academy-id "q-256610" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [0,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-3,\\,3]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $-2.621$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-3)(3)^{3}-1 & 3(-3)^{2}(3)^{2}+4(3)^{3} \\\\ 3(-3)^{2}-2(3) & -2(-3)+2(3)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2}(3)^{3}-(-3)+{3}^{4}-3 \\\\ (-3)^{3}-2(-3)(3)+{3}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-163 & 351 \\\\ 21 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-163)\\cdot 12-351\\cdot 21}[\\begin{aligned}12 & -351 \\\\ -21 & -163\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9327}[\\begin{aligned}3537 \\\\ -6967\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.621 \\\\ 2.253\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-256610/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256610"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "56656957-dee5-5336-b9ab-df078dda7207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "fc70e868-0dbc-5ca9-83b8-caf65e36f430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "49fe46fe-6368-59e3-aa0f-c6869911b10c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256586/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer-field/choices ["q-256586/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" "q-256586/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "b98813f6-4d8f-58dd-a2b0-15f843fe6b3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b55fab2c-649a-59ad-896d-91240fcbb627" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256586/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-256586/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-3/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "a8eff71b-ee71-5a07-97c9-ed8a921627fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "82b7d895-b540-5cd1-bd0a-71a73bd4a7c8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256586/field-3/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-256586/field-3/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-4/291312251ba1606e431c151401fd064e84abe983a40c9c345e2863ff091a3705" :answer/id #uuid "de27efff-ce0a-5ff0-b858-b6e9e29ebb5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0ee7af-17bc-5832-aea4-9c5464a1caa5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256586/field-4/291312251ba1606e431c151401fd064e84abe983a40c9c345e2863ff091a3705" :answer-field/choices ["q-256586/field-4/291312251ba1606e431c151401fd064e84abe983a40c9c345e2863ff091a3705"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256586" :question/id #uuid "d00d997d-f1e0-59ac-b8d9-0ebd105edd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-256586" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[2,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-12$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-8$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1.9$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(2)^{3} & -3(2)^{2} \\\\ 2(2)-2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4}-{2}^{3} \\\\ {2}^{2}-(2)(2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}32 & -12 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.9 \\\\ 2.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-256586/field-1" "q-256586/field-2" "q-256586/field-3" "q-256586/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8f