\n\n| $[$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $2$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-5y+8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-5y+8) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}-5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}-5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-5y+8 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}-5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-5y+8 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}(1) & (1)^{3}-5 \\\\ (1)^{2} & 2(1)(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}(1)-5(1)+8 \\\\ (1)(1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-256549/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "b84bd99f-f0aa-5704-b3e1-192be94fc36e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :