b/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256549" :question/id #uuid "1e6f9b3f-c122-5df2-be00-eca0cb3b8398" :question/math-academy-id "q-256549" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[-2,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $-2$ | $-6$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $-1$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+2(1) & 2(-2)-2(1) \\\\ {1}^{2}-1 & 2(-2)(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+2(-2)(1)-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ (-2)(1)^{2}-(-2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-256549/field-1" "q-256549/field-2" "q-256549/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256549"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-255856/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "7a8f1d57-60b6-595b-b44a-2c61accdaa04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186202926 :answer-field/correct "q-255856/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-255856/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-255856/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b15d60cd-c700-5bf2-84b5-f4b12346c512" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186202923 :answer-field/correct "q-255856/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-255856/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-255856/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9aee7007-4d99-54f1-ae40-e46566ac8175" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186202925 :answer-field/correct "q-255856/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-255856/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-255856/field-4/053d7e69e862f6d79297f36aa8bda869faab0e82c418b42ca3fad9876d5499a7" :answer/id #uuid "f7385174-942d-5217-b692-d21b8510f1a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.833" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186202924 :answer-field/correct "q-255856/field-4/053d7e69e862f6d79297f36aa8bda869faab0e82c418b42ca3fad9876d5499a7" :answer-field/choices ["q-255856/field-4/053d7e69e862f6d79297f36aa8bda869faab0e82c418b42ca3fad9876d5499a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-255856"] :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-3}} | $3$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.833$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(1) & 2(1) \\\\ 3(1)^{2} & 3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-2 \\\\ (1)^{3}+(1)^{3}-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.833 \\\\ 0.833\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-256548/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "3fce816e-df91-5fde-b22f-6a768ddea34c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256548/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c76d854-1961-57a6-be9b-2733e3df2c73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256548/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-256548/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256548/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "4f0b48f7-2060-5105-a19e-4f2d94366fc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256548/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6f81cf-0a92-56cd-a70f-ed9950284e86" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256548/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-256548/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256548/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "02bf40d6-b8f0-5780-a85a-7d9ed9931708" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-256548/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "be36069e-0650-564d-81e1-3d3c67f88251" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-256548/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-256548/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-256548" :question/id #uuid "5e671a6c-acb1-57e8-957b-d79d3aefc9ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-256548" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[1,\\,0]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3(0) & 2(0)-3(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 3(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3}-3(1)(0)+{0}^{2}-1 \\\\ {1}^{2}+{0}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-256548/field-1" "q-256548/field-2" "q-256548/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-256548"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-16569"] :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2] \\times [0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/0f5dda7937b1ffed646730040efe11464d46ef1f3ec6ae5dacf84075bd5be01b.png)\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T.$" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/c3ec90119193ca9c0cb886bc7b7c698eb155e6a801fd267c0a17a0d329eff84d.png)\n\nStarting with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/eff9e5b8ab46002bd82fa42d59f60b1bb78ca04228d7c73b52d131d222ccb894.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-x-2 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(2)-1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}(2)-1-2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2}-2+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-16570"] :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3] \\times [-2,0] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/891b79a977ea7d9f4062d3614d7001ade71cfc10de54fcbae6608eebed7e8d1a.png)\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 0 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf{x}_1 = \\big[ 3, \\: -1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/95e234c5c9b32e1ea965f1cd184563699fbd2793d51a9d1e4ec96ffb4e42dc50.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-4 \\\\ x+{y}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(3) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(-1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}+(-1)-4 \\\\ 3+(-1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{13}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}2.308 \\\\ -0.846\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-16571"] :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/c3c2b2e7a9d5b2b8f76499b0ad62d628f9d79bc7c41b6bcaf9926df4f368cdbf.png)\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\nJ_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/13f9eb9850462317be5a5c7da22fe53ee89b24d9ce1a9226c2d0ee1e6355ba19.png)\n\nStarting with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2(1) & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-(1)^{2} \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/d5eb5a91fdd96bf6ca50539d2119a97ce9e5600b3114f708cda4d95a047e8b81.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.75 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
